  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY NIGHT THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 1233 AM MDT Mon Oct 12 2026\n\nTemperatures will be cooler for Tuesday as the upper low approaches \nthe 4 corners region and mid level temperatures cool another  2-4 \ndegrees C, especially along the Continental Divide.  Although the \nsystem does not look as deeply moist as the previous ones, forcing \nwill increase through Tuesday night as the consensus of the models \nlifts the upper low from the 4 corners region into NE CO at varying \ntimings.  GFS has trended towards the EC and Canadian but is still \non the faster side of the envelope with the upper low filling and \nlifting out of NE CO Wednesday Night. EC and Canadian are \naround 12 hours slower, hanging the upper low back over CO \nthrough Wednesday Night before filling and lifting it \nnortheastward into NE CO by mid day Thursday as energy out west\ncuts off and retrogrades over the southwest U.S. and the Baja \nPeninsula. \n\nModels are in fair agreement though Tuesday with showers continuing \nalong the Continental Divide, and dry and breezy conditions \nprevailing across the southeast plains.  Once the system begins to \neject eastward, there are still some differences with QPF across the \nsoutheast plains. Eventually a cold front will drop through the \nsoutheast plains bringing noticeably cooler temperatures for \nWednesday and a chance for rain showers.  Will need to watch \nWednesday closely as depending on the position of the front and \ntiming of moisture advection in southerly flow ahead of it, there \ncould be a window for some strong storms across Baca county \nshould a slower cold front timing prevail and a ribbon of higher\nCAPE can set up across the area. Odds are better to the south \nand east but it will bear watching as cooling aloft with the \nupper low could steepen mid level lapse rates sufficiently. \nOtherwise, EC and Canadian camps suggest some light to moderate \nsnowfall along the Continental Divide, especially Wed as snow \nlevels drop below pass levels with a few inches of accumulation \npossible. Would like to see a better moisture fetch with this \nsystem to have higher confidence in impactful snowfall amounts \nand the need for highlights along the Continental Divide, but \nthere will be at least some light to moderate accumulations at \ntimes across Wolf Creek and Monarch Passes. \n\nThe southeast plains could see some more widespread overrunning \nprecipitation if the slower closed low solution pans out on \nWednesday, with lingering chances into Thursday as the flow \nbecomes north to northeasterly on the back side of the sheared \nout upper low/trough axis extending from NE CO to the closed low\nover southern CA. Precipitation amounts on Thursday will be \nlighter. Of course if the faster GFS solution pans out, then \nprecipitation chances will end sooner Thu as the system departs \nmore quickly to the northeast. Either way, it will be cooler and\nmore Fall like for all of the southeast plains Wednesday and \nThursday. We will likely see a freeze (and possibly a hard \nfreeze) across the San Luis Valley Wednesday and Thursday \nnights as NBM shows widespread 80-90% probabilities of 32 \ndegrees or colder, and a 30-60% chance of 28 or colder both \nnights. The southeast plains could see a frost or light freeze \nThursday and/or Friday nights as NBM shows odds of temperatures \nbelow 32 ranging from 20 to 30 percent across southern areas to \nas high 50 percent across Northern El Paso and Kiowa counties. \nAreas along/north of highway 50 may have the best chances of \nneeding frost/freeze highlights one or both nights. \n\nOtherwise dry and mild weather returns Friday through next weekend \nwith temperatures returning to near climatological normals by the \nweekend.  -KT",
